{"skill":{"slug":"accli","displayName":"Apple Calendar CLI"},"version":{"version":"1.0.0","createdAt":1767644556460,"changelog":"","changelogSource":null,"license":null,"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":5438,"sha256":"1b9096fee2d5fe1720045aa7b80e7a0600f0743a2b50cf7b24e46fe5b6cdf22c","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":1830,"sha256":"4ee88d9ad77724dad387e71f4a7982ac65159f9dad8f63adc8a8166ffd13771a","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"clean","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789015023321,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"5c6e90df76c4abc823078913c1d00b57d14d7ef9f14e11a3cf32935c6e7d83fa","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/5c6e90df76c4abc823078913c1d00b57d14d7ef9f14e11a3cf32935c6e7d83fa","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"clean","verdict":"benign","normalizedStatus":"clean","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: accli\nVersion: 1.0.0\n\nThe skill bundle is classified as benign. The `SKILL.md` provides instructions for installing and using `accli`, a command-line tool for managing Apple Calendar on macOS. All commands and examples are directly related to calendar operations (listing, creating, updating, deleting events, checking free/busy times). While the installation step (`npm install -g @joargp/accli`) introduces a supply chain risk from the external npm package, the instructions within the skill bundle itself do not exhibit any malicious intent, data exfiltration, persistence mechanisms, or prompt injection attempts against the agent. The instructions are clear, aligned with the stated purpose, and even include safety warnings like confirming deletions with the user.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779159633765},"skillspector":{"status":"clean","normalizedStatus":"clean","score":17,"severity":"LOW","recommendation":"SAFE","issueCount":2,"checkedAt":1789015023321},"llm":{"status":"clean","verdict":"benign","normalizedStatus":"clean","confidence":"medium","summary":"This is a straightforward Apple Calendar helper, but it depends on a globally installed third-party npm CLI that users should verify first.","dimensions":[{"detail":"The macOS-only JavaScript for Automation requirement is coherent with controlling Apple Calendar locally.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"ok"},{"detail":"Installation uses an unpinned global npm install for @joargp/accli, so the runtime code is external to the reviewed artifact and may change over time.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The trigger is broad for calendar-related requests and the skill can read sensitive schedule data, but it stays within Apple Calendar operations and includes guidance to use reasonable date ranges and confirm creates/deletes.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"note"},{"detail":"No hidden persistence, background worker, tool hijacking, or memory-writing behavior appears in the artifact; the only persistence described is an explicit default-calendar configuration.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The documented capabilities match the stated Apple Calendar purpose: listing calendars, reading events, creating/updating/deleting events, and checking free/busy time.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"ok"}],"guidance":"Install only if you are comfortable granting a third-party CLI access to Apple Calendar. Prefer verifying the npm package and source, pinning a reviewed version, using a non-admin account, limiting calendar/date ranges, and confirming any create, update, or delete action before it runs.","findings":"[SQP-1] expected: The trigger text is broad, but still limited to calendar-related tasks and coherent with a calendar-management skill; this supports user guidance rather than a Review verdict by itself.\n[SQP-2] expected: Calendar data can be sensitive and the artifact does not give a strong privacy warning, but the access is central to the stated purpose and the commands expose date-range and calendar scoping options.\n[T08] expected: Installing a CLI dependency is expected for this wrapper skill, but the unpinned global npm command is a real supply-chain caution users should review before installing.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789015023321}}}}}